WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.400
PROFESSOR: This brings me to
my third example of divination

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.830
in the early Christian period.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.760
And this example is an actual
Christian divination book

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.980
that we have here at Harvard.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.230
It dates likely from the
Fifth or the Sixth Century.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.210
This book is very, very tiny.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:29.280
It measures less than three
inches in height and in width.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.800
It could have fit in the
palm of someone's hand.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.000
It's made of vellum.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.970
It's been tested, and it
was sheepskin that was used.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:40.200
And the writing on it is a very
fine uncials, a fine handwriting

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.540
that kind of imitates the
handwriting that would

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:45.210
have been used for a biblical text.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.490
It's a text that in
its very paleography,

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:53.610
in the very script that it uses,
claims a kind of authority for itself.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.670
A sophisticated style of writing.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:01.680
The text is titled The
Gospel of the Lots of Mary.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.140
And within it, it
contains multiple oracles.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.070
And we're not really sure how it would
have been used, to tell you the truth.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.740
What's clear is that someone
held it in their hand.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.880
And if you look closely at the text,
you can see that it's been well-thumbed.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:20.520
The middle of each page is dark
with the stains of sweat and dirt

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.850
from a ritual expert who
would have held it and thumbed

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:29.190
through the pages trying to find an
answer to the petitioner's questions.

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:32.970
We can imagine the possibility that
a petitioner in the Fifth Century

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.370
or in the Sixth Century in Egypt--

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:38.790
because the text is written in
Coptic, the common language in Egypt

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:39.879
at that time--

00:01:39.879 --> 00:01:42.420
that the petitioner would have
come to the ritual expert, who

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.970
would have held a text like
this in his or her hands

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:49.950
and leafed through it to find an
answer to the petitioner's troubles.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:53.940
It's a practice not unlike
that we found at Cremna,

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.800
although at Cremna it
was a public practice

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.650
in a public square of
throwing the astragalus

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.230
and trying to find an
answer from the god.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:05.910
In this case, we have a practice with a
small book, a kind of bibliomancy where

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:09.000
a given page would tell you
the answer to the question

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.810
that you held in your heart and that
you discussed with the ritual expert

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:13.960
about your future.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:15.150
Let me read you the
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH],

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.850
or the introduction, of the text,
so that you can have a sense

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:22.890
of the concerns that an early Christian
might have brought to a ritual expert.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:28.270
"The Gospel of the Lots of Mary,
the mother of the Lord Jesus Christ,

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:32.490
she to whom Gabriel the arch
angel brought the good news.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:38.130
He will go forward with his whole
heart, will obtain what he seeks.

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.680
Only do not be of two minds."

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.590
The handwriting that looks
like a biblical script.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.960
The reference to Mary, the
mother of Jesus Christ.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:48.930
These give a kind of
authority to the text,

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:52.080
a kind of religious sanction to it.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.540
The first Oracle reads this way.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:55.920
"Oh, human.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.320
With their tongues
they love you greatly,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.810
but their heart is far from you.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:05.670
And you know what happened to you before
today that you almost lost your life.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:10.980
In any case, they certainly
lie in wait for you."

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.060
The oracles in this small
little divination device

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.200
often talk about not fearing,
about trusting in God.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.920
They give pieces of divine
advice for the Fifth

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.130
or the Sixth Century
Egyptian Christian who

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:30.000
came to the ritual expert seeking
some understanding of what

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.170
might happen in his or her
future, seeking some guidance

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:37.880
in his or her path for the present.

